{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788971592,"nonce":"0NHMoLRaZhg+nFWnEDir39aAlR0=","message":"SCOPING PASS, 2026-09-09. Read-only against decompiled 1.6, `fingerkill/`, shipped Royalty/Core defs and `Source/AutoRimmer/`. No bench was touched.\n\nThe premise is right and this is the largest of the four: `Pawn_AbilityTracker` is unreached by any of the 139 verbs, and so is `Pawn_PsychicEntropyTracker` — a grep for `psychicEntropy`, `psyfocus`, `psylink` and `abilities` over `PawnSerializer.cs`, `PawnSafe.cs` and `DigestVerb.cs` returns **zero hits**, so the agent cannot even see that a pawn has a psylink. The gate list in the body is the best-researched of the four, and it is still missing about half the chain. Nine corrections, seven additions, and the open question.\n\n=====================================================================\nCORRECTIONS\n=====================================================================\n\n**1. Namespaces.** `Ability` and `Pawn_AbilityTracker` are `namespace RimWorld` — `RimWorld/Ability.cs`, `RimWorld/Pawn_AbilityTracker.cs`, not `Verse/`.\n\n**2. `Ability.CanCast` is a property returning `AcceptanceReport`, not a bool**, and `Psycast` overrides it with a branch that will mislead anyone reading it as \"checks psyfocus\":\n```\nif (def.EntropyGain \u003e float.Epsilon)\n{\n    if (pawn.GetPsylinkLevel() \u003c def.level \u0026\u0026 def.level \u003e 0) return false;\n    return !pawn.psychicEntropy.WouldOverflowEntropy(def.EntropyGain);\n}\nif (def.PsyfocusCost \u003e pawn.psychicEntropy.CurrentPsyfocus + 0.0005f) return false;\n```\n**The two are mutually exclusive.** An ability with any entropy gain never has its psyfocus checked in `CanCast` at all. Only `GizmoDisabled` and `Verb_CastPsycast.ValidateTarget` check both. A verb that used `CanCast` as its gate would let a Bloodbond fire with no psyfocus.\n\n**3. `Ability.GetGizmo()` does not exist.** It is `GetGizmos()` → `IEnumerable\u003cCommand\u003e`, and `Psycast` **overrides it and ignores `def.gizmoClass`**, always yielding a cached `Command_Psycast`. Immaterial to the act path (see addition E — the verb should not touch gizmos) but the citation is wrong.\n\n**4. `Ability.ValidateTarget` does not exist.** `Ability` has only `ValidateGlobalTarget(GlobalTargetInfo)`. The per-target validation the issue wants is `Verb_CastPsycast.ValidateTarget` → `Verb_CastAbility.ValidateTarget`, and **`Verb_CastAbility` does NOT call `Verb.ValidateTarget`**, so the base's extra-faction / innocent-animal / venerated-animal clauses never run for an ability.\n\n**5. `Ability.Verb` is `verb`, lowercase, declared type `Verb`** — not `Verb_CastAbility`. And it reads the **private `verbTracker` field**, bypassing the lazy property, so `ability.verb` on an `Ability` that has not run `Initialize()` throws. Cast defensively.\n\n**6. `QueueCastingJob` returns `void` — there is no success signal, and it can fail SILENTLY.**\n```\npublic virtual void QueueCastingJob(LocalTargetInfo target, LocalTargetInfo destination)\n{\n    if (!CanQueueCast || !CanApplyOn(target)) return;                    // silent, no message\n    if (verb.verbProps.nonInterruptingSelfCast) { verb.TryStartCastOn(verb.Caster); return; }\n    ShowCastingConfirmationIfNeeded(target, delegate { … pawn.jobs.TryTakeOrderedJob(GetJob(target, destination), JobTag.Misc); });\n}\n```\nTwo overloads only: `(LocalTargetInfo, LocalTargetInfo)` and `(GlobalTargetInfo)`. There is no `(LocalTargetInfo, GlobalTargetInfo)`. **Consequence: the verb must read back what happened** (`GetJob` builds `JobDefOf.CastAbilityOnThing` with `job.verbToUse = verb` and `job.ability = this`; check `pawn.jobs.curJob.ability == ability`) rather than reporting the call as the result. Same class as `4087644` and as `nepo-order`'s `order-did-not-take`.\n\n**7. `Ability_PsyfocusCost` and `Ability_EntropyGain` are `StatDef`s, not comps and not XML fields on `AbilityDef`.** `StatDefOf.Ability_PsyfocusCost` / `StatDefOf.Ability_EntropyGain`, read out of `AbilityDef.statBases`:\n```\npublic float EntropyGain  =\u003e statBases.GetStatValueFromList(StatDefOf.Ability_EntropyGain, 0f);\npublic float PsyfocusCost =\u003e statBases.GetStatValueFromList(StatDefOf.Ability_PsyfocusCost, 0f);\n```\nThere is no `Ability_PsyfocusCost.cs`. **And the flat `def.PsyfocusCost` is not the number to publish** — `Ability.FinalPsyfocusCost(target)` lets any comp with `props.OverridesPsyfocusCost` replace it per target. The gizmo layer uses the flat stat; `Verb_CastPsycast.ValidateTarget` uses `FinalPsyfocusCost`. Publish both when they differ.\n\n**8. `Pawn_PsychicEntropyTracker` has no `PsylinkLevel`.** Use `PawnUtility.GetPsylinkLevel(pawn)` (sums every `Hediff_Psylink.level`) or `tracker.Psylink.level`. The tracker does have `CurrentPsyfocus`, `EntropyValue`, `MaxEntropy`, `WouldOverflowEntropy(float)`, `PsychicSensitivity`, and two the issue does not know about and needs: **`MaxAbilityLevel`** and **`PsyfocusBand`**, which produce a refusal all their own (B6 below). Also `public const float PsyfocusCostTolerance = 0.0005f;` — the epsilon every comparison uses.\n\n**9. `CompUseEffect_LearnAbility` does not exist; the psytrainer comp is `CompUseEffect_GainAbility`, and its psylink-level check is a WARNING, not a gate.**\n```\nif (!p.health.hediffSet.HasHediff(HediffDefOf.PsychicAmplifier))\n    return \"PsycastNeurotrainerNoPsylink\".TranslateWithBackup(…);\nif (p.abilities != null \u0026\u0026 p.abilities.abilities.Any(a =\u003e a.def == Props.ability))\n    return \"PsycastNeurotrainerAbilityAlreadyLearned\".Translate(…);\n```\nLevel-too-low is only `ConfirmMessage`. So a psylink-1 pawn CAN consume a level-6 psytrainer and simply cannot cast the result — which makes the read half of this issue load-bearing rather than a convenience.\nRelated: **30 psycasts ship, not 29** (`Royalty/Defs/AbilityDefs/Abilities.xml` has 31 concrete `defName`s, one of which — `Speech` — is not a `Psycast`), and **none of the psytrainers ship as XML.** All are implied defs from `ThingDefGenerator_Neurotrainer.ImpliedThingDefs()`, one per `AbilityDef` where `IsPsycast`, **including modded ones** — so fingerkill's three get generated too on a non-VPE bench. A worker greping `Data/` for `Psytrainer_` will find nothing.\n\n**Also correct as written, briefly:** `Pawn.abilities` is a real public field; `Ability.CooldownTicksRemaining` is real; `verbProperties.targetParams` is real and all eight named `TargetingParameters` fields exist; `Verb.CanHitTarget(LocalTargetInfo)` is real; the \"three separate refusals must not be collapsed into one\" instruction is right and is if anything understated — there are **five** psycast-specific refusals, not three (B3–B7 below).\n\n=====================================================================\nFINGERKILL — the def claims\n=====================================================================\n**Bloodbond: every number in the issue is correct.** Level 5, psyfocus 0.30, entropy 20, range 24.9, LOS required, pawns only, `hostile False`, `displayGizmoWhileUndrafted True`, comp `Fingerkill.CompAbilityEffect_Bloodbond`. (The field is `requireLineOfSight`, no `s`.) Its `Valid` refuses: non-pawn, non-humanlike, hostile-to-caster, already bonded to a *different* caster. `Apply` does add `Bloodbond_TargetLink` + `Bloodbond_SourceLink`, a `BloodbondShock` thought, and `TryProvokeRetaliation` → `Bloodbond_MurderousRage`, or **`Tantrum`** when the victim `WorkTagIsDisabled(Violent)`. The acceptance should say \"murderous rage, or Tantrum for a violence-incapable victim\", or it will fail on a pacifist prisoner.\n\n**Fingerkill: every number correct.** Level 6, psyfocus 0.01, entropy 0, range 9999, `requireLineOfSight False`, pawns and animals, `hostile True`. Two things the issue's framing gets wrong:\n  * **Range 9999 is not \"anywhere\".** `Verb.TryFindShootLineFromTo` refuses a different map outright, and `VerbProperties.AdjustedRange` clamps `EffectiveRange` to `map.weatherManager.CurWeatherMaxRangeCap` when the weather sets one. In fog the range collapses. `EffectiveRange`, not `verbProps.range`, is what the read must publish.\n  * **The order in the acceptance is backwards.** `ApplyFingerkillTo` takes the **caster's** part FIRST (bail if `SacrificeLedger.FindNextSacrifice` is null), then severs the victim's four limbs, then a `DamageDefOf.Bomb` explosion, then 25 random cuts, and **destroys the heart LAST**. The \"fixed sequence\" is the caster's 42-entry `SacrificeLedger.Sequence` (fingers → toes → arm/leg subparts → kidneys → eyes → liver → spine, fatal), re-derived every call with no stored state.\n\n**Voidpull is NOT \"the same family\", and this matters for the target argument.** It is a **cell-target summon**: `canTargetPawns False`, `canTargetLocations True`, entropy 60, psyfocus 0.90, warmup 12s, `cooldownTicksRange 120000~120000`, three comps, and a **`confirmationDialogText`**. So the bench's three modded psycasts already cover both target shapes plus the confirmation path — a good acceptance set, but \"same family\" would send a worker looking for a pawn target that does not exist.\n\n**A real defect in the mod, found while checking this and worth its own issue:** Bloodbond and Voidpull set `displayGizmoWhileUndrafted True` but **neither sets `disableGizmoWhileUndrafted`, whose `AbilityDef` default is `true`** and which `PsycastBase` does not override. So the gizmo is shown while undrafted and then disabled with `\"AbilityDisabledUndrafted\"` (\"must be drafted.\"). Every vanilla def that wants undrafted casting sets both. `grep -rn \"disableGizmoWhileUndrafted\" fingerkill/` → no hits. That is a fingerkill bug, not an AutoRimmer one; filing it separately is the right move, and until it is fixed the acceptance must draft the caster or the two undrafted-castable psycasts refuse.\n\n=====================================================================\nADDITIONS\n=====================================================================\n\n**A. THE GATE CHAIN IS EIGHTEEN CHECKS AT THE GIZMO LAYER AND NINE MORE AT THE TARGET LAYER.** The issue names six. In order:\n\n*Disable layer* — `Psycast.GizmoDisabled` then `Ability.GizmoDisabled`:\n  B1 `PsychicSensitivity \u003c ε` → `CommandPsycastZeroPsychicSensitivity`\n  B2 `def.level \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 GetPsylinkLevel() \u003c def.level` → `CommandPsycastHigherLevelPsylinkRequired`\n  B3 `def.PsyfocusCost + TotalPsyfocusCostOfQueuedPsycasts(pawn) \u003e CurrentPsyfocus + 0.0005` → `CommandPsycastNotEnoughPsyfocus`\n  B4 **`def.level \u003e pawn.psychicEntropy.MaxAbilityLevel`** → `CommandPsycastLowPsyfocus` — **a distinct refusal the issue does not have.** Psyfocus is banded (`PsyfocusBandPercentages {0,.25,.5,1}`, `MaxAbilityLevelPerPsyfocusBand {2,4,6}`): below 25% psyfocus you cannot cast above level 2 **even if you can afford the cost**. This is the single most confusing psycast rule in the game and the agent will hit it constantly.\n  B5 `def.EntropyGain \u003e ε \u0026\u0026 WouldOverflowEntropy(EntropyGain + TotalEntropyFromQueuedPsycasts(pawn))` → `CommandPsycastWouldExceedEntropy`\n  B6 `CanCooldown \u0026\u0026 OnCooldown \u0026\u0026 (!cooldownPerCharge || charges == 0)` → `AbilityOnCooldown`\n  B7 `UsesCharges \u0026\u0026 charges \u003c= 0` → `AbilityNoCharges`\n  B8 any `comps[i].GizmoDisabled(out reason)` — **all `AbilityComp`s, not just effect comps**\n  B9 `!CanCast.Accepted` → `CanCast.Reason`, **frequently empty**: pocket map, vacuum, planet layer, and several bare `false`s\n  B10 `pawn.GetLord()?.AbilityAllowed(this)` rejected\n  B11 `!pawn.Drafted \u0026\u0026 def.disableGizmoWhileUndrafted \u0026\u0026 GetCaravan() == null` → `AbilityDisabledUndrafted`\n  B12 `pawn.DevelopmentalStage.Baby()` → `IsIncapped`  ← **`PawnSafe` Class C; see addition D**\n  B13 `pawn.Downed` → `CommandDisabledUnconscious`\n  B14 `pawn.Deathresting` → `CommandDisabledDeathresting`\n  B15 `def.casterMustBeCapableOfViolence \u0026\u0026 WorkTagIsDisabled(Violent)` → `IsIncapableOfViolence`\n  B16 `!CanQueueCast` → `AbilityAlreadyQueued`\n\n*Target layer* — `Verb_CastPsycast.ValidateTarget`:\n  E1 out of range → `AbilityOutOfRange` · E2 in range but unhittable (LOS, `mustCastOnOpenGround`, psychologically invisible, apparel) → `AbilityCannotHitTarget` · E3 `EffectiveRange \u003c= 0 \u0026\u0026 !pawn.CanReach(target, Touch, NormalMaxDanger)` → `AbilityCannotReachTarget` · E4 `!CanApplyPsycastTo(target)` → `AbilityTargetPsychicallyDeaf` · E5 any `EffectComps[i].Valid(target, showMessages)` → comp-supplied · E6 boss target with no `canTargetBosses` → `CommandPsycastInsanityImmune` · E7 sensitivity · E8 entropy with queue · E9 `FinalPsyfocusCost(target) + queued \u003e CurrentPsyfocus + 0.0005`.\n  Plus, separately, the targeter applies `verb.targetParams.CanTarget(...)`.\n\n**The queued-cast terms (B3, B5, E8, E9) are the ones a naive implementation drops** and they are exactly what an agent that queues casts will hit: `PsycastUtility.TotalPsyfocusCostOfQueuedPsycasts` / `TotalEntropyFromQueuedPsycasts` read `pawn.jobs.curJob` and `pawn.jobs.jobQueue`. Publishing \"psyfocus 0.6, cost 0.3, castable\" while two casts sit in the queue is a false yes.\n\n**B. `CanApplyOn` AND `Valid` ARE NEVER BOTH CALLED, AND THE `CanApplyOn` PATH SILENTLY SKIPS COMPS.** This is the trap in this issue.\n```\npublic virtual bool CanApplyOn(LocalTargetInfo target)\n{\n    if (effectComps != null)               // PRIVATE FIELD, null until the EffectComps property is touched\n        foreach (CompAbilityEffect c in effectComps)\n            if (!c.CanApplyOn(target, null)) return false;\n    …\n}\n```\n`QueueCastingJob` gates on this. `Verb_CastAbility.ValidateTarget` gates on `Valid` and **never calls `CanApplyOn`**. So:\n  * A comp that overrides only `Valid` is not consulted by `QueueCastingJob`.\n  * A comp that overrides only `CanApplyOn` is not consulted by the targeter.\n  * **On a freshly loaded save, `effectComps` is null and `CanApplyOn` skips every comp silently.** The verb must touch `ability.EffectComps` once before trusting it.\nThis is live on the bench: `CompAbilityEffect_Fingerkill.CanApplyOn` only checks `target.Pawn != null` and does **not** re-check the sacrifice ledger, while its `Valid` and `GizmoDisabled` do — so an exhausted ledger is caught at the gizmo and at the targeter, and not at `QueueCastingJob`.\n\n**C. `Pawn_AbilityTracker.AllAbilitiesForReading` IS A REBUILD-ON-READ SHARED LIST — `PawnSafe` Class E, and worse.** When `allAbilitiesCachedDirty`, it does `allAbilitiesCached.Clear(); AddRange(abilities);` then appends hediff / equipment / apparel / mutant / royalty / ideo-role abilities, and in the mutant-whitelist branch **reassigns the field to a brand-new list**. It returns the live internal list. Anything during an enumeration that fires `Notify_TemporaryAbilitiesChanged()` (hediff add/remove, equipment or apparel swap, ideo role change) and then re-reads the property clears the collection under the `foreach` — the Collection-was-modified bug 2.1 shipped live. And a stored reference goes stale silently rather than throwing in the whitelist branch. **Snapshot into our own list before iterating**, exactly as `PawnVerbs` does with `AllPawnsSpawned`. `AICastableAbilities` returns the shared `tmpAbilities` field and must never be held.\n`pawn.abilities.abilities` (the plain public field) is eagerly initialised and safe, but it holds only innate/learned abilities — it misses hediff, equipment, apparel and role abilities, so it is not the list to read.\n\n**D. `pawn.DevelopmentalStage.Baby()` is in the gate chain (B12) and is a `PawnSafe` Class C ban.** It routes through `Pawn_AgeTracker.CurLifeStageIndex`, which on a stale cache can `CheckChangePawnKindName()` (rename the pawn) and `AddAndRemoveDynamicComponents`. `PawnActs`' header already draws the line — where the WIDGET calls a lazy-init getter, the player's click calls it too, so an ACT verb may. **The READ verb may not**, and must substitute a guarded equivalent (`ageTracker.AgeBiologicalYears`, which `PawnSafe` already sanctions) and say so. Same split as `orders`/`prioritize` versus the observers.\n\n**E. THE VERB MUST NOT TOUCH GIZMOS, and `Command_Ability.DisabledCheck` is not the route.** `Command_Ability.Disabled` calls `DisabledCheck()`, which calls `ability.GizmoDisabled(out reason)` — so the gizmo adds nothing the `Ability` does not already give, and getting one requires `Pawn_AbilityTracker.GetGizmos()`, which is selection- and `DebugSettings`-sensitive and constructs cached `Command` objects. **Call `ability.GizmoDisabled(out reason)` directly.** (`Command_Ability.GizmoOnGUI` does add one click-time gate not in `GizmoDisabled` — `(ability.Casting || QueueOrder.IsDownEvent) \u0026\u0026 !ability.CanQueueCast` — but `CanQueueCast` is B16 and covers it.)\n\n**F. `ShowCastingConfirmationIfNeeded` MAKES THE JOB ASYNCHRONOUS, AND ONE BENCH PSYCAST USES IT.** `QueueCastingJob` wraps the `TryTakeOrderedJob` in a callback passed to `ShowCastingConfirmationIfNeeded`, which consults each comp's `ConfirmationDialog(target, act)` and then `def.confirmationDialogText`. **Voidpull sets `confirmationDialogText`.** When a dialog is produced, `Find.WindowStack.Add(...)` runs and the job is created only if someone presses the button — so `QueueCastingJob` returns having done nothing, and the run is now under a force-pausing window. Whether that window is answerable depends on its class (`dialog-choose` handles a `Dialog_NodeTree`; a `Dialog_MessageBox` is **not** answerable by any shipped verb — see my comment on `d318d4a`).\n**Ruling: pre-check the confirmation the same way `d318d4a`'s `use` must** — read `def.confirmationDialogText` and each comp's `ConfirmationDialog` before calling, publish the text as a disclosed `dialogs_skipped`-style field, and reproduce the callback body (`pawn.jobs.TryTakeOrderedJob(ability.GetJob(target, dest), JobTag.Misc)`) rather than letting the window open. `GetJob` is public, which makes this clean.\n\n**G. THE STATE THE AGENT NEEDS TO BUDGET A CAST IS NOT PUBLISHED ANYWHERE.** Zero hits for `psychicEntropy` / `psyfocus` / `psylink` across `PawnSerializer.cs`, `PawnSafe.cs` and `DigestVerb.cs`. Without `psylink_level`, `psyfocus` (current + band + `MaxAbilityLevel`), `entropy` (current, max, and the `PsychicEntropyGain` stat that scales it) and `psychic_sensitivity`, a `castable:false` with reason `CommandPsycastLowPsyfocus` is unactionable — the agent cannot tell how long to wait or how much to meditate. **This block ships with the read half**, and it belongs to `abilities`, not to `pawn` (see the settled question). `MaxEntropy` is `pawn.GetStatValue(StatDefOf.PsychicEntropyMax)` — a `StatWorker.GetValue`, i.e. `PawnSafe` Class F, accepted and already sanctioned for the `pawn` drill-down.\n\n**H. `queue`, and what `TryTakeOrderedJob` does here.** `GetJob` hands back a plain `Job`, and `QueueCastingJob` calls `TryTakeOrderedJob(job, JobTag.Misc)` with no `requestQueueing` — same shape as `attack` (`bc2250b`). Reproducing the callback (addition F) makes `queue` honourable through `PawnActs.TakeOrder`, which is the right answer since the psycast queue accounting (`TotalPsyfocusCostOfQueuedPsycasts`) exists precisely because vanilla expects players to stack casts. Use `TakeOrder` and publish `order_effect`.\n\n**I. Do not call `Ability.Activate` or `verb.TryStartCastOn`.** Both skip the job, the warmup, `Ability.WarmupTick`'s continuous re-validation of `CanApplyOn`, and the queue accounting. `fingerkill/Source/MpBridge.cs` registers `CompAbilityEffect_Fingerkill.SyncedStartFury` with MP and its own comment says Voidpull needs no registration \"because the cast job is the synced player action\" — i.e. the mod itself treats `QueueCastingJob`'s job as the sync boundary. Going around it desyncs, the same argument DESIGN's 2026-09-09 Nepo entry makes about `ScheduleShipment`.\n\n**J. RNG is on the shared stream in the cast path.** `Ability.PreActivate` and `Ability.CooldownTick` both call `def.cooldownTicksRange.RandomInRange`; comp effects roll too (`CompAbilityEffect_Fingerkill` picks 25 random cut locations). That is fine on an ACT verb — the player's click burns the same rolls — but it disqualifies any of it from the READ. The read must never call `Activate`, `PreActivate`, or a comp `Apply`. (`Voidpull`'s pawn generation is already wrapped in `Rand.PushState`/`PopState`.)\n\n**K. `Psycast.Activate` subtracts psyfocus UNCONDITIONALLY.** `if (def.EntropyGain \u003e ε \u0026\u0026 !TryAddEntropy(def.EntropyGain)) return false;` — entropy is a hard abort; then `OffsetPsyfocusDirectly(0f - FinalPsyfocusCost(target))` with **no check**, clamped at 0. So a cast that slips past the gates spends psyfocus it did not have and floors. Report `psyfocus_after` from a read-back, never computed.\n\n=====================================================================\nOPEN QUESTION SETTLED: a separate `abilities` verb, not a `pawn` section\n=====================================================================\nFour arguments, all from the tree's own shape.\n\n**1. Every gate-evaluating discovery surface in the tree is already its own verb, and the `pawn` sections are uniformly gate-free.** `PawnSerializer.AllSections` is `identity · state · needs · mood · health · skills · apparel · equipment · inventory · schedule · work · area · relations` — thirteen descriptions of state, none of which evaluates whether an action would be accepted. The four surfaces that DO are all separate verbs paired with an act verb: `surgery-options`/`surgery-add`, `bill-options`/`bill-add`, `orders`/`prioritize`, `comms-targets`/`comms-call`. `MedicalBillVerbs`' own header states the rule: *\"This is the discovery surface for `surgery-add`, and it exists for the same reason `orders` does.\"* An abilities read with a `castable` verdict and eighteen named refusals is that shape exactly, and **a pawn is already an `IBillGiver` whose surgery options could have been a `pawn` section and deliberately are not.**\n\n**2. A `pawn` section cannot take a target, and half the chain is target-dependent.** The gates split cleanly: B1–B16 are target-free, E1–E9 need a `LocalTargetInfo`. `pawn` takes `id`, `sections`, `opinions`, `opinion_cap` and has no place to put one. `abilities {pawn, target?}` answers both questions — \"what could this pawn cast at all\" and \"could it cast X at Y\" — and the second is the one that actually precedes a cast.\n\n**3. Cost, and `pawn` defaults to ALL sections.** `PawnVerbs.PawnDetail` fills every section when `sections` is absent. An abilities section would put an 18-gate × N-abilities evaluation — including `GetStatValue` calls for entropy and sensitivity, and `PsycastUtility`'s two job-queue walks — on every `pawn \u003cid\u003e` call in the run. The class comment already notes the full result is ~4KB and that sections exist so a caller paying for context can ask for two.\n\n**4. The safety split runs along the same line.** B12 (`DevelopmentalStage.Baby()`) is a `PawnSafe` Class C ban for observers and legal for an act verb. Keeping the ability read in its own verb keeps `PawnSerializer` — which is pure observation — free of a gate chain that has to reason about which of its clauses an observer may run.\n\n**So: `abilities {pawn, target?}` ships as its own verb with `ability-cast`, and `pawn` gains no section.** Two vocabularies for one fact is what `927be4f` is about; do not add a thin `abilities` list to `pawn` as well. The per-ability row carries `def`, `label`, `level`, `psyfocus_cost` (flat and `FinalPsyfocusCost` when they differ), `entropy_gain`, `range` (**`EffectiveRange`**, not `verbProps.range` — weather clamps it), `requires_los`, `cooldown_ticks_remaining`, `charges`/`uses_charges`, `target_params` (the real `TargetingParameters` flags), `castable`, and when not castable the gate name plus the game's reason. The caster-level psychic block from addition G rides on the envelope, not per row.\n\n=====================================================================\nSIZE AND DEPENDENCY\n=====================================================================\n**Independent of the other three in this batch, and independent of `d318d4a`.** The issue's \"paired with the `use` verb, which is how a pawn LEARNS most of them\" is true as motivation and is **not** a build dependency: every psycast on this bench is already reachable through `dev:add-hediff` (psylink) and the fingerkill psycasts are learnable by other routes. Ordering them `d318d4a` → `ae84a07` is nicer for the bench story; nothing blocks.\n\n**Size: two files, ~600–800 lines, the largest of the four.** A new `AbilityVerbs.cs` at ~500 (the eighteen-clause disable chain, the nine-clause target chain, the target argument accepting a pawn id and a cell, the confirmation pre-check, the `GetJob` reproduction, the read-back) plus ~150 for the psychic-state block, and ~40 in `PawnSafe.cs` for the guarded `AllAbilitiesForReading` snapshot and the `AgeBiologicalYears` substitution for B12, which belongs there rather than in the verb file.\n\n**Risky part 1: `CanApplyOn` skipping every comp when `effectComps` is null** (addition B). A verb that calls `QueueCastingJob` on a just-loaded save gets past a comp refusal it printed as `castable:false` one call earlier, because the read touched `EffectComps` and the act did not — or vice versa. It fails intermittently, in a direction that depends on call order.\n\n**Risky part 2: the two mutually-exclusive branches in `Psycast.CanCast`** (correction 2). It is the member whose name says \"can I cast this\", and using it as the gate silently drops the psyfocus check for every ability with entropy — which on this bench is Bloodbond and Voidpull, two of the three acceptance targets.\n\n**Risky part 3: `AllAbilitiesForReading`** (addition C). It is the obvious accessor, it has the right name, and it hands back a live list that the game clears and refills.\n\n**Acceptance note:** the bullet \"casting Bloodbond on a prisoner produces … the murderous-rage mental state\" needs \"or `Tantrum` if the victim is incapable of violence\", and the Fingerkill bullet needs its order corrected (caster's part first, heart last). Both will otherwise fail against a correct implementation.","files":null}]}